AUDIO: 'Okay, so vendor neutral MIT licensed on your own hardware without a runtime bill. These are all things that Claude managed agents doesn\'t have. Also, the new OpenAI agent and API or Gemini'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agents doesn\'t have them. But all of these things are the things that the agent system I\'m going to show you today actually has. Today I want to talk about a way to run managed agents fully'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'locally or in your own private cloud with any model that you want, adding in any tools that you want, skills that you want, MCPs that you want, and being able to do it all really easy without you'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'having to go into low-level code and tinker with every tool call or every skill that you add. Today, I want to talk about a self-managed agent execution harness called True Forge. Now, before we get into it, this video'

[00:50] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "Harnesses" text on screen with abstract lines.
AUDIO: 'is sponsored by True Foundry, the creators of True Forge, but everything I\'m showing you today is in an open-source repo. There\'s no paid tier in anything that you\'ll see. And the ask from them is basically that if you find'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this useful and interesting and you would like to see them work on it more, just go and start the repo. That\'s it. I wor with True Foundry earlier on in the year and made a video about their gateway platform and it seems to me that'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as they\'re developing things for enterprise, they\'re also looking at how they can make some of these cool tools available to the open-source community as well. In this video, I want to try and untangle that a little bit. I want'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to talk about how you should be thinking about the different kinds of harnesses that are out there and how you can be using them for your different kinds of projects. So let\'s start with the word because I think this is where a lot of'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the confusion is. When most people hear the words agent harness right now they tend to think of coding harnesses. That\'s things like claude codeex pi om open code a whole bunch of these and'

[01:52] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "Tedious" text with abstract icons, representing complexity.
AUDIO: 'those are harnesses right they wrap a model they give it a file system a shell some tools and they run the loop for you but they\'re really built for one job right which is writing code. The second'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of harness that\'s out there at the moment is what I would call the assemble itself framework. So this is Langraph, deep agents perhaps agent SDK or ADK and these give you all the pieces and you'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write a loop and you wire up everything yourself, right? You decide how the state gets persisted, how approvals work and so on. And I\'ve done quite a number of videos on those over the years, but'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nowadays can be actually really tedious to use a lot of those frameworks if you\'re trying to build your own harness. The third kind and the one that\'s most recent is what I actually just made a video about recently, which is the whole idea of a hosted runtime harness. So,'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is what people are calling managed agents. So, you\'ve got Claude managed agents, you\'ve got Gemini managed agents, and I made a whole video about this talking about the different kinds'

[02:54] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "Hosted runtime harness" with two cloud icons, one with an asterisk, the other with a rainbow, both locked.
AUDIO: 'of managed agents that are out there. But the whole idea with those is that you define an agent, give it a model, almost certainly it\'s going to have to be the provider\'s model. You\'ll have to give it tools, which again will be often'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provided for you, and a system prompt. And then that company\'s going to run the loop. So session, tool calls, sandboxes, approval, all of that is handled. You just hit an API. And that third category'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can be really nice to use, right? I\'ve built with it, etc. But here\'s the thing. Up until pretty recently, that category really meant you only had one choice in that it has to be hosted with the actual provider. And perhaps even'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worse than that, you\'re often just tied down to certain models that may just be not a great fit for what you\'re trying to do. So, True Forge is an open-source version of that third kind. And the'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'readme puts it really nicely. Building an agent is easy, but running one well is not. The running it well part is all about streaming, session persistence, tool servers, sandboxes, approvals, a'

[03:59] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Iceberg graphic with a money bag, illustrating hidden costs.
AUDIO: 'UI. That\'s all the stuff that really honestly most people don\'t want to write. And it\'s the stuff that managed agents have been charging you a premium for. True Forge is that layer as a repo'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s fully open- source, MIT licensed that you can clone and then run with any particular model that you want to. All right. So the question should be why does it matter what kind of managed'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent I\'m using? And this is the bit that I think many people even myself at times have gotten wrong that you tend to think of the cost of all of this as just being related to which model that you\'re'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using and how much you\'re paying per token. And then often you don\'t think about the harness and all the plumbing that is actually going on around this. But when you start running managed agents at volume, you start to realize'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that actually that harness really is often the key thing because it\'s that harness that is actually deciding how many tools calls you make, what tool calls you make, in what order, how it\'s'

[05:03] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: TrueFoundry logo and "trueforge // Open-source, vendor-neutral harness" text.
AUDIO: 'going to actually decide what goes in the context window. Remember, really what we\'re paying for is whatever\'s in the context window. So, if you\'ve got lots of tool calls in there, if you\'ve got tool bloat, right, where you\'ve just'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got so many tool definitions and stuff in there, and you\'re not using progressive disclosure and things like that, you\'re going to run up a much bigger bill than you would with a very efficient harness. And this is where the'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'True Foundry team actually put out a benchmark that shows all of this. They took 14 tasks from DevRev\'s enterprise bench, the kind where the agent has to join data across a CRM, an issue'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tracker, a document store, multiple MCP servers, all with the same prompt. And they ran three trials each and used a blind LLM judge. And here\'s the thing, they didn\'t even go for cheaper models.'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They actually ran it on Opus through three different harnesses. And you can see from this diagram here, they basically showed with the actual true forge harness that even using the same model, they were able to get the cost'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'down by 30% with the same amount of accuracy. And that\'s because Claude managed agents was using 10 million tokens per run on average versus only 3.8 million tokens on the true forge.'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And of course, when they moved that to a model like GLM 5.2, suddenly the model cost came down. the whole thing now was down 75% compared to the original claude managed agents version. So even if we'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skip the GLM 5.2 and just look at the same model, same answers for roughly a third of the tokens, that was all harness decided. So the fact that True Forge is actually open source means that'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can pick any model that you want here. You can decide if you want to go for a more expensive clawed model or open AI model, etc. But you can also go for much cheaper open models like I\'m'

[07:01] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: News headlines about Chinese AI labs using Claude and China-Based AI Companies conducting industrial-scale distillation.
AUDIO: 'going to show you in here. So, if you just take one thing away from this video, it\'s this. The harness, not the model, is usually what\'s driving your agent costs. And the harness is the part that you\'ve actually got a lot of'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control over, even if you\'re not running your own models with your own GPUs, etc. Now for me I would say while the cost savings here are the headline for me the lockin thing is probably a bigger deal than a lot of people are watching just'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the park sweep we\'ve seen a number of different news items about what user data these people are actually training on and how they\'re actually using user data and you\'ve got things like data retention if you want to have zeroday'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data retention you cannot use clawed managed agents if your agent definition lives inside a hosted runtime then and the system prompt, the tool wiring, the approval rules, the session history, all'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of that sits on someone else\'s product. And perhaps that\'s fine up until the day you want to try a different model or you want to try a different sandbox or you want to try a different configuration,'

[08:06] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing a central TrueForge icon connected to various other icons (models, tools, etc.) with a "/v1/chat/completions" endpoint.
AUDIO: 'etc. You don\'t want to get locked into these services where you become dependent on them for a lot of things beyond the model. We\'re already seeing open models get so good that really high levels of intelligence are now being'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'democratized so you can access it over lots of different providers. Even if you have objections or legal things around sending your data to certain countries, the cool thing now is with open weights models, there are providers popping up'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everywhere. They\'re running the same weights with the same API endpoints. And I hear this a lot from people building inside larger companies. They often have a mandate to run things on prem or at least inside their own cloud account and'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a hosted agent system just isn\'t going to get through procurement through compliance no matter how good it is. So they fall back into a category of trying to write the entire loop themselves and that can also become both an inefficient'

[09:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: TrueForge documentation page, showing `npx @truefoundry/trueforge@latest` command for local installation.
AUDIO: 'and expensive option. And then of course if you do have some local hardware that you can run some local models on we\'re already seeing the smaller models get better and better and this whole system allows you to integrate with things like'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
